   "output": "Read only the page below. Read no other file, and do not run commands or browse. Answer each question from the page alone. When the page does not say, answer \"Not stated\".\n\n<<<PAGE\n# Renaming rooms in the Kestrel directory\n\n## 1. Identify the room by its key\n\nKestrel is a fictional directory of shared work rooms. Each room has a room key and a display name. These serve different purposes: the key identifies the room, while the display name is the human-readable label people see.\n\nA room receives its key when it is created. That key never changes and is never reused for another room. Room owners can change the display name. A rename therefore changes the room\u2019s visible label while preserving its identity.\n\nFor example, suppose an owner renames a room from \u201cPlanning\u201d to \u201cProject Planning.\u201d The display name changes, but the room retains the key assigned when it was created. Both names describe the same room at different points in time.\n\nThis distinction matters when building room links. Saved links identify rooms by their keys. When someone opens one, Kestrel looks up the key and displays the room\u2019s current name. A link built from a display name can stop finding the room after a rename because that name has changed.\n\nIn the storage schema, `room_ref` is the key column and `title_text` is the display-name column. Read these fields according to their roles: `room_ref` holds the room\u2019s permanent identity; `title_text` holds its changeable label.\n\n## 2. Verify the rename with a saved link\n\nVerify a rename by opening a room link saved before the name changes. This checks whether the link still reaches the same room and whether Kestrel shows its new display name.\n\nUse this sequence:\n\n1. Before renaming the room, save a room link that identifies it by its key.\n2. Rename the room.\n3. Open the saved link.\n4. Check that it reaches the same room.\n5. Check that the room shows the new name.\n\nBoth checks matter. Reaching the same room verifies that the saved link continues to identify it through the unchanged key. Seeing the new name verifies that Kestrel displays the room\u2019s current label when the link opens.\n\nKeep the original saved link for this check. Opening it after the rename exercises the behavior the lesson describes: a link saved under an earlier name should still reach the same room and show its new name.\nPAGE>>>\n\nQuestions:\n(a) What stays the same when a room is renamed?\n(b) Can a link built from a display name stop working after a rename?\n(c) Which link should you open to verify that a rename preserved access?\n\nReply with only JSON: {\"a\": \"...\", \"b\": \"...\", \"c\": \"...\"}. Start the answer to (b) with Yes, No, or Not stated.",
